// Details for the machine learning framework plate, part 4: operators, kernels, tensors, memory, devices.
ATLAS.details("ml-framework", {
  "operator-schema": {
    s: "A declarative signature for each operator: its name, argument and return types, and annotations for mutation and aliasing, from which bindings, dispatch and documentation are generated.",
    d: "A schema is a short text line per operator, giving argument names and types, defaults, whether an argument is mutated, and whether an output aliases an input. Code generators read the schema list to produce host-language bindings, dispatcher stubs, autograd wrappers and shape-checking boilerplate. Alias and mutation annotations are what functionalization and compilers rely on to reason about side effects. Overloads share a name but differ in signature. The difficulty is evolution: a schema is a public contract, so changes need backward-compatibility rules, and an incorrect alias annotation causes subtle miscompilation.",
    k: ["One declarative source of truth vs hand-written declarations in each layer: a single schema keeps every layer consistent automatically, but the generator becomes critical infrastructure.", "Strict typed arguments vs permissive conversion: strict types catch mistakes and speed dispatch, while permissive conversion is friendlier to users but hides errors."],
    p: ["An operator that mutates its input but is declared pure gets reordered by the compiler, and results change silently.", "Adding an argument without a default breaks every serialized graph that calls the operator."],
    v: "Check that every registered kernel's actual behavior matches its schema's mutation and alias annotations, by running each operator under a checker that detects undeclared writes and undeclared aliasing, and that all bindings regenerate cleanly from the schema list.",
    dep: ["operator-registry"]
  },
  "operator-registry": {
    s: "The global table mapping operator names to their schemas and to the implementations registered for each dispatch key, populated at load time by the framework and by extensions.",
    d: "At library load time, each library registers schemas under a namespace and then registers kernels for particular keys, such as one device or one transformation. Extensions add implementations for new devices or operators without modifying the core. The registry is queried to build the dispatch table and by tools, such as exporters and compilers, to list operators and their properties. Registration order and duplicates must be handled deterministically. The difficulty is initialization: tens of thousands of registrations run at startup, adding to load time, and a registration that is accidentally dropped by the linker fails only at call time.",
    k: ["Static registration at load time vs lazy registration on first use: static gives complete tables for tools but slows startup; lazy starts faster but makes the set of operators incomplete until used.", "Open registry for extensions vs a closed core set: open allows new hardware and operators without forking, but permits conflicting overrides."],
    p: ["A kernel registered in a library that the linker removes as unused is missing, and the operator errors for that device at run time.", "Two extensions register the same operator and key, and which one wins depends on load order."],
    v: "Enumerate the registry and check that every schema has an implementation or fallback for each supported key, and that loading extensions in any order yields the same dispatch table. Registering a duplicate should error clearly.",
    dep: ["operator-schema","dispatch-keys"]
  },
  "dispatcher": {
    s: "The runtime component that, on each operator call, computes which implementation to run from the call's arguments and the active modes, and invokes it, possibly passing through layers in sequence.",
    d: "For each call, the dispatcher gathers a set of keys from the tensors involved, from their devices and layouts to flags such as requiring gradients, combines them with a thread-local set of active modes, and picks the highest-priority key. That key's kernel runs, and may redispatch to the next key after doing its own work, as autograd does before calling the device kernel. The lookup is a table index kept cheap, since it runs for every operator. Fallbacks supply behavior for operators with no registered kernel. The difficulty is overhead, since a microsecond matters when millions of calls run, and understanding the order in which layers run.",
    k: ["Multiple dispatch on all arguments vs on the first argument: multiple dispatch handles mixed devices and modes correctly, but makes lookup and reasoning more complex.", "Layered redispatch vs a single flat table of implementations: layering lets autograd, tracing and batching compose in any combination, but each layer adds call overhead."],
    p: ["An operator called on tensors from different devices dispatches to a surprising kernel instead of raising a clear error.", "Expensive logic added in a dispatch layer makes every operator slower, and small operators become host-bound."],
    v: "Call one operator under combinations of device, dtype and active modes, record the sequence of keys visited with a dispatch trace, and confirm it matches the documented priority order. Microbenchmark dispatch overhead per call.",
    dep: ["dispatch-keys","operator-registry","fallbacks-and-composites","tensor-type"]
  },
  "dispatch-keys": {
    s: "Small tags that name an implementation axis, such as device, layout, dtype family, autograd, tracing and batching, ordered by priority and held as a bit set on every tensor and in thread-local state.",
    d: "Each tensor carries a bit set of keys derived from its properties: its device and layout, whether it requires gradients, and any wrapper roles it plays. Thread-local include and exclude sets add or remove keys, which is how modes such as no-grad and tracing take effect. Keys are ordered from outermost behaviors, such as batching and autograd, to innermost backend keys that select the actual kernel. Adding a new device or transformation means adding a key. The difficulty is the fixed capacity of the bit set and the combinatorics: each new key interacts with every existing one.",
    k: ["Separate keys for each concern vs one combined type tag: separate keys compose freely, but their count is bounded and their ordering needs care.", "Thread-local mode state vs per-tensor state only: thread-local modes switch behavior for a whole block cheaply, but surprise code that expects tensors to carry all the information."],
    p: ["A key ordered after another causes autograd to see or miss an operation, giving wrong or missing gradients.", "Thread-local mode state set in one thread is absent in worker threads, so backward or data loading behaves differently."],
    v: "For a tensor with each combination of properties, print its key set and confirm it matches the expected keys. Enter and leave a no-grad mode and confirm the key set changes in that thread only.",
    dep: ["tensor-type","device-abstraction"]
  },
  "fallbacks-and-composites": {
    s: "Default implementations used when no kernel is registered for a key: composites built from other operators, and backend fallbacks that run on the host or raise a clear error.",
    d: "A composite operator is implemented in terms of other operators, so it works on every backend that supports its parts, and differentiates automatically. Backends then override only operators where a native kernel is faster. A fallback handles a whole key: a backend with incomplete coverage may copy to host, run there and copy back, with a warning, or may raise an error naming the operator. Fallbacks make bringing up a new device possible with a small kernel set. The difficulty is silent slowness from host round trips, and composites that are correct but too slow on a given device.",
    k: ["Composite default vs required native kernel per backend: composites give instant broad coverage and automatic gradients, but may be slower or less numerically careful than a native kernel.", "Silent host fallback vs hard error: fallback keeps programs running, while an error makes missing coverage obvious and avoids silent slowness."],
    p: ["A missing operator on an accelerator falls back to the host, and a training loop slows tenfold with only one warning in the logs.", "A composite built from reduced-precision pieces loses accuracy compared with a single kernel that accumulates in higher precision."],
    v: "Run every operator on a backend with fallbacks counted and list any that fall back or error. For composites, compare outputs and gradients against a native kernel or a double-precision reference.",
    dep: ["dispatcher","operator-registry","lowering-and-decomposition"]
  },
  "batching-transforms": {
    s: "Function transforms that turn code written for one example into code for a batch, by mapping it over a dimension, without the user writing batched code by hand.",
    d: "A batching transform wraps tensors in batched tensors that carry a hidden batch dimension, and registers a dispatch layer where each operator is rewritten to act on the full batch, using a per-operator batching rule. Composing it with autograd gives per-sample gradients, Jacobians and ensembles of models without loops. Operators without a rule fall back to a slow loop over the batch. Transforms nest and compose with differentiation in either order. The difficulty is rule coverage, handling data-dependent control flow, which cannot be batched, and the interaction of in-place operations with the hidden dimension.",
    k: ["Automatic batching by transform vs manual batched code: a transform is correct by construction and gives per-sample gradients cheaply, but may be slower than hand-tuned batched kernels.", "Loop fallback for missing rules vs error: a loop keeps code working but silently loses the speedup; an error is explicit but blocks use."],
    p: ["An operator with no batching rule silently runs as a loop, and a vectorized function is no faster than a host-language loop.", "Data-dependent branching on a batched tensor cannot be expressed and raises a confusing error."],
    v: "Map a function over a batch with the transform and compare against a loop over examples, in outputs and gradients. Check the profile for the absence of a per-example loop and a speedup over the loop version.",
    dep: ["dispatch-keys","dispatcher","reverse-mode-tape"]
  },
  "custom-operator-api": {
    s: "The public way for users to add an operator with their own kernel, schema, derivative and shape rule, so that it works with eager mode, autograd, tracing and compilation like a built-in.",
    d: "A user declares a schema in a namespace, registers a kernel for each backend, optionally registers a derivative, and a meta kernel that computes output shapes without running. With those pieces, the dispatcher routes calls, autograd attaches the user's backward, and capture and compilation treat the operator as an opaque node with known shapes. Kernels can be written in the host language, in a device kernel language or in native code built as an extension. The difficulty is completeness: an operator missing its meta kernel or its derivative works in eager mode but fails under export, compile or training.",
    k: ["Opaque custom operator vs expressing it with built-in operators: opaque allows a hand-tuned kernel, but the compiler cannot fuse or optimize through it; composition lets the compiler see inside.", "Registered through the dispatcher vs called directly as a function: registration works with every transform, while a direct call is quick to write but invisible to autograd and capture."],
    p: ["An operator without a meta kernel makes compilation fail or fall back to a graph break at every call.", "A kernel that returns a view or mutates an input without declaring it in the schema is miscompiled by functionalization."],
    v: "Register a custom operator with all pieces and run it under eager mode, gradient checking, tracing, export and compilation, comparing outputs with a reference implementation each time. A test suite supplied by the framework automates these.",
    dep: ["operator-schema","operator-registry","meta-kernels","custom-autograd-functions","accelerator-kernels"]
  },
  "meta-kernels": {
    s: "Kernels registered for a data-free device that compute only output shape, dtype and strides for each operator, used for shape inference, fake tensors and building huge models without memory.",
    d: "A meta kernel takes input metadata and returns output tensors with correct metadata and no storage. It validates arguments, so errors in shapes are caught early with the same messages as real kernels. They underlie fake tensors, deferred parameter initialization, shape propagation for export and compilation, and memory estimation. Most are generated or shared with real kernels' shape logic to prevent drift. The difficulty is exactness: output strides and aliasing must match real kernels, data-dependent output shapes cannot be computed without data, and thousands of operators need coverage.",
    k: ["Shape logic shared with real kernels vs separate meta implementations: sharing prevents drift but couples shape code to kernel code; separate implementations are flexible but can disagree.", "Report an upper bound for data-dependent shapes vs refuse: a bound lets compilation proceed, but wastes memory; refusing is exact but blocks capture."],
    p: ["A meta kernel returns contiguous strides where the real kernel returns transposed ones, and a compiled graph is wrong.", "A meta kernel that skips an argument check lets a bad shape pass tracing and fail only at run time."],
    v: "Run each operator on real and meta tensors for a grid of shapes, dtypes and strides and confirm that metadata matches exactly. Confirm that error cases raise on both paths with the same message.",
    dep: ["operator-registry","tensor-type","shape-and-strides"]
  },
  "cpu-kernels": {
    s: "Operator implementations for host processors, using vector instructions, cache blocking and multi-threading, with runtime selection of the best instruction set available.",
    d: "Kernels are written once as templates over vector width and compiled several times for different instruction sets, with the best chosen at startup by a CPU capability check. Elementwise and reduction operations are parallelized across a thread pool in chunks sized to keep each thread busy without false sharing. Matrix operations call vendor libraries or blocked implementations that tile for caches. Strided and broadcast inputs are handled by an iterator that collapses dimensions. The difficulty is the long tail: hundreds of operators, many dtypes, and denormal numbers, plus ensuring the same results across instruction sets.",
    k: ["Runtime instruction-set dispatch vs compiling for one target: runtime dispatch ships one binary for all machines, but multiplies build size and test matrix.", "Parallelize inside each operator vs run operators on separate threads: intra-operator uses all cores on large tensors, but offers nothing for small ones and contends with data loading."],
    p: ["Intra-operator threads oversubscribe the cores when combined with data loader workers, and every step slows down.", "Different vector widths change the summation order, so results differ between machines in the low bits."],
    v: "Run each operator across all supported instruction-set paths and compare against a scalar reference within dtype tolerance. Scale thread count on a large tensor and confirm near-linear speedup until memory bandwidth saturates.",
    dep: ["host-thread-pools","tensor-type","vendor-math-libraries","dtype-system"]
  },
  "accelerator-kernels": {
    s: "Operator implementations for massively parallel devices, organized as grids of thread blocks with explicit use of on-chip memory, written to saturate memory bandwidth or arithmetic units.",
    d: "A kernel launches a grid of blocks, each with many threads that share fast on-chip memory and synchronize within the block. Elementwise kernels use grid-stride loops and vectorized loads; reductions combine within a thread, then a warp, then a block, then across blocks; indexing and sorting kernels use specialized algorithms. Matrix multiply uses hardware matrix units on tiles staged through shared memory. Kernels are templated over dtypes and compiled for several device generations. The difficulty is performance portability, race conditions, numerical differences among reduction orders, and coverage of the operator long tail.",
    k: ["Handwritten kernels vs generated kernels: handwritten reach peak performance on important operators, while generation covers many fused variants with less effort.", "Atomic accumulation vs deterministic two-stage reduction: atomics are simple and fast but order-dependent; two-stage reductions give repeatable results at a cost."],
    p: ["An atomic-add based kernel gives different results each run, breaking reproducibility in a way that looks like a model bug.", "A kernel tuned for large inputs runs a single block on small ones, leaving most of the device idle."],
    v: "Compare each kernel against a host reference over sizes at block and warp boundaries, and run under a memory and race checking tool. Plot achieved bandwidth against the device's peak for memory-bound kernels.",
    dep: ["device-abstraction","streams","kernel-templates","vendor-math-libraries","caching-allocator"]
  },
  "vendor-math-libraries": {
    s: "Hardware makers' tuned libraries for dense linear algebra, FFTs, sparse operations and random numbers, which the framework calls instead of writing its own.",
    d: "The matrix multiply library is the most important: it provides routines for many shapes, dtypes and batched forms, selecting among kernels by heuristic and exposing options for algorithm choice and workspace. The framework wraps each call, translating tensors' strides to the library's layout conventions, handling transposition flags, choosing precision and accumulation modes, and passing the current stream and a workspace. Handles are created per thread and stream. Tuned libraries reach near-peak throughput, which the framework cannot match for large matrices. The difficulty is the version coupling, nondeterminism in some algorithms, and workspace memory.",
    k: ["Calling a vendor library vs generating matrix multiplies: libraries are fast and maintained by the hardware maker, but are opaque to fusion; generated code can fuse epilogues at somewhat lower peak.", "Library heuristics vs benchmarked algorithm selection: heuristics add no startup cost, while benchmarking finds faster algorithms but adds startup and nondeterminism."],
    p: ["Workspace allocated by the library bypasses the framework's allocator and causes unexpected out-of-memory errors.", "A library upgrade changes the selected algorithm and shifts numerical results across a release."],
    v: "Compare library results for varied shapes, strides and dtypes against a high-precision reference, and compare throughput with the device's peak. Confirm the library runs on the framework's current stream by checking the profile.",
    dep: ["streams","caching-allocator","dtype-system","shape-and-strides"]
  },
  "vendor-primitive-libraries": {
    s: "Vendor libraries of neural-network primitives, such as convolution, normalization, pooling and attention, with multiple algorithms per operation and fused variants for supported patterns.",
    d: "These libraries offer convolution with many algorithm choices, from direct to transform-based, each with different speed, workspace and numerical behavior. Normalization, activation and recurrent primitives are provided in forward and backward forms, as are some fused patterns, such as convolution plus bias plus activation. Newer versions expose a graph interface where the framework describes a subgraph and the library compiles it into fused kernels. The framework provides descriptors for tensors and operations, selects an algorithm, and manages workspace. The difficulty is the number of combinations and version-specific behavior.",
    k: ["Use vendor primitives vs the framework's own kernels: vendor primitives are tuned per device generation, but are closed and may differ numerically between versions.", "Describe fused subgraphs to the library vs fusing in the framework compiler: library fusion uses vendor internals, while compiler fusion is transparent but leaves hardware-specific tricks unused."],
    p: ["The fastest algorithm requires a large workspace, and enabling it raises peak memory enough to cause out-of-memory failures.", "Algorithms differ in numerics, so the benchmarked-fastest choice changes training results from run to run."],
    v: "For each primitive, compare forward and backward outputs against a reference across shapes, strides and dtypes, and list which algorithm was selected and its workspace use. Confirm throughput against vendor-published figures.",
    dep: ["layout-optimization","streams","caching-allocator","dtype-system"]
  },
  "attention-kernels": {
    s: "Fused kernels for scaled dot-product attention that compute softmax and the two matrix multiplies in tiles on chip, avoiding the quadratic intermediate matrix in device memory.",
    d: "Naive attention materializes a score matrix whose size grows with the square of sequence length, making memory traffic dominate. Fused kernels process keys and values in blocks, keeping a running maximum and sum for an online softmax so the full matrix never exists. The backward pass recomputes scores from saved row statistics instead of storing them. Variants handle causal masks, sliding windows, grouped heads and cached keys and values for decoding, where one query attends to a long cache. The difficulty is the number of variants and the tuning of tile sizes for each device.",
    k: ["Fused tiled attention vs separate matrix multiplies: fused saves memory and bandwidth and allows long sequences, but is harder to write and less flexible about masks and variants.", "Recompute in backward vs store scores: recompute keeps memory linear in length, at roughly a third more compute."],
    p: ["Online softmax accumulated in low precision loses accuracy for long sequences with a large dynamic range.", "A rarely used mask form falls back to the slow unfused path and a long-sequence job runs out of memory."],
    v: "Compare fused output and gradients against the naive implementation in double precision for varied lengths and masks. Measure memory growth with length and confirm it is linear rather than quadratic.",
    dep: ["accelerator-kernels","kernel-templates","kv-cache","dtype-system"]
  },
  "fused-optimizer-kernels": {
    s: "Kernels that apply an optimizer update to many parameters in a single launch, reading and writing each element once instead of launching several small operations per parameter.",
    d: "A multi-tensor kernel receives a list of pointers and sizes to parameters, gradients and state, and a single launch updates all of them, with each block assigned a chunk of one tensor. Fusing the update rule into one pass reads each element once and writes it once, instead of reading it for each elementwise operation. Gradient unscaling, finite-checking and clipping can be fused into the same pass. The speedup is large for models with many small tensors, where launch overhead otherwise dominates. The difficulty is keeping the fused numerics identical to the reference update.",
    k: ["Multi-tensor fused kernel vs per-tensor operator loop: fused removes thousands of launches and memory passes, but needs a hand-written kernel per optimizer and cannot fuse with arbitrary user logic.", "Fused update with master weights in the kernel vs separate cast: fusing saves a pass, but ties the kernel to one precision recipe."],
    p: ["A fused kernel rounds differently than the loop version, so results shift when users switch the implementation.", "A tensor list exceeding the kernel's argument limit is split incorrectly and some parameters are not updated."],
    v: "Run fused and reference updates from identical state for many steps and compare parameters elementwise within tolerance. Profile a step and confirm kernel count equals one per chunk of the parameter list instead of one per parameter.",
    dep: ["optimizers","accelerator-kernels","parameters-and-buffers","loss-scaling"]
  },
  "kernel-templates": {
    s: "Reusable parameterized kernel skeletons, generic over dtype, tile shape, layout and epilogue, from which many specialized kernels are produced at build time or just in time.",
    d: "A template supplies the structure of a kernel, such as the tiling and software pipelining of a matrix multiply or the reduction tree of a normalization, while parameters select element types, tile sizes and fused operations at the beginning and end. Instantiating it for the supported combinations produces a family of kernels, selected by shape and device. It makes new fused variants cheap to add and keeps performance-critical structure in one place. The difficulty is build time and binary size from combinatorial instantiation, and the expertise required to write the skeleton correctly.",
    k: ["Build-time instantiation vs just-in-time instantiation: build-time kernels are ready at startup but inflate binary size; just-in-time covers any combination but costs compile time on first use.", "General template vs specialized kernel: a template covers many cases at slightly reduced performance, while a specialized kernel is fastest for one important case."],
    p: ["A new tile configuration is instantiated without testing, and an alignment assumption fails only at shapes seen in production.", "Instantiating every combination of dtype, layout and tile bloats the shipped binary to gigabytes."],
    v: "Instantiate each supported parameter combination, run it over shapes at tile boundaries against a reference, and record the binary size contributed by each family to keep build growth visible.",
    dep: ["accelerator-kernels","dtype-system","vendor-math-libraries"]
  },
  "tensor-type": {
    s: "The core data type: a small header giving shape, strides, dtype, device and offset over a reference-counted storage, plus metadata used by autograd and dispatch.",
    d: "A tensor object holds sizes, strides, a storage offset, a dtype, a device, a pointer to shared storage and the set of dispatch keys. Autograd metadata, such as the gradient field, the producing node and the requires-gradient flag, is attached lazily. Copying a tensor copies the header, not the data. Reference counting shares the storage among views, and frees it when the last reference disappears. Because tensors are created millions of times per step, the header is kept small and allocation-light. The difficulty is the number of invariants: contiguity, alias relationships and version counts all derive from the header and storage together.",
    k: ["Lightweight header over shared storage vs a self-contained array: sharing makes views and slicing free, but introduces aliasing hazards and complicates lifetime.", "Autograd metadata inside the tensor vs in a side table: inside is fast to reach but enlarges every tensor, while a side table keeps inference tensors small at some lookup cost."],
    p: ["Holding a small view keeps the entire large storage it points into alive, so memory is not released as expected.", "Assuming a tensor is contiguous in custom kernels reads wrong elements for transposed or sliced inputs."],
    v: "Create a tensor, take views, and confirm they share storage and report consistent shape, stride and offset. Free all but a small view and confirm the full storage remains allocated until the last reference is dropped.",
    dep: ["storage","shape-and-strides","dtype-system","device-abstraction"]
  },
  "shape-and-strides": {
    s: "The metadata that maps an index tuple to a memory offset: a size per dimension and a stride per dimension, which lets transposes, slices and broadcasts be views without copying.",
    d: "The element at index i, j, k lies at offset plus i times stride zero plus j times stride one plus k times stride two. A transpose swaps strides, a slice changes offset and size, an expand sets a stride to zero so one element appears repeated, and a reshape of a compatible layout only edits metadata. A tensor is contiguous when strides match row-major order. Kernels either require contiguous inputs or iterate with strides. Memory format choices, such as channels-last, are just different stride patterns. The difficulty is the number of kernels that silently assume contiguity and the cost of making copies.",
    k: ["Strided views vs always contiguous tensors: strides make many operations free, but every kernel must handle arbitrary strides or pay for a copy.", "Reshape that copies when needed vs reshape that must be a view: automatic copying is convenient, but hides performance cost and breaks aliasing assumptions."],
    p: ["A kernel that assumes contiguous input is passed a transposed view and silently computes on the wrong elements.", "A zero-stride expanded tensor is written to in place, and one write changes every position that aliases it."],
    v: "For a set of tensors with transposed, sliced and expanded strides, read every element through the index formula and compare with the materialized contiguous copy. Run each kernel on strided input and confirm it matches the contiguous result.",
    dep: ["tensor-type","storage","views-and-aliasing"]
  },
  "dtype-system": {
    s: "The set of element types a tensor can hold, such as floating, integer, boolean and complex formats of several widths, with rules for conversion and for the result type of mixed operations.",
    d: "Each dtype defines its size, kind, range and rounding. Operations on mixed dtypes use promotion rules: the result type is chosen by category and width, with scalars and zero-dimensional tensors treated differently from full tensors so a literal does not widen a half-precision tensor. Kernels are compiled for each supported dtype by templates. Conversion rounds to nearest, saturates or wraps depending on the pair. Low-precision floating formats exist in several variants. The difficulty is promotion rules, which must be precise and stable, and the large matrix of operator and dtype combinations needing kernels and tests.",
    k: ["Implicit type promotion vs explicit casts required: promotion is convenient and matches array-programming habits, but silently changes precision and memory use.", "Few supported dtypes per operator vs all of them: restricting keeps testing and binary size manageable, but fails on valid user combinations."],
    p: ["A float literal combined with a half-precision tensor is promoted unexpectedly, and the whole computation runs in a wider format.", "Integer division and overflow wrap silently in an index computation and produce wrong but valid-looking positions."],
    v: "Tabulate the result dtype for every pair of dtypes under binary operations and compare with the documented promotion table. Round-trip values through each conversion and confirm rounding and saturation behavior at range limits.",
    dep: ["tensor-type"]
  },
  "views-and-aliasing": {
    s: "Operations that return a new tensor header sharing the original storage, so a write through one name is visible through every other name for the same memory.",
    d: "Slicing, transposing, reshaping when possible, expanding and selecting produce views that point to the same storage with different shape, strides and offset. A view remembers its base. Writes to a view change the base and all other views, which makes in-place operations powerful but hazardous. The schema marks which operators return views, so autograd can track them: modifying a view in place rewrites the history of its base, which autograd handles by regenerating graph nodes. Compilers must model aliasing exactly. The difficulty is that aliasing is invisible in the program text and interacts with autograd, threads and the compiler.",
    k: ["Views by default vs copies by default: views make slicing free and enable in-place updates, but create hidden dependencies; copies are safe but cost memory and time.", "Tracking the base of every view vs tracking only at mutation: tracking always allows correct autograd on later mutation, but adds memory and bookkeeping to every view."],
    p: ["An in-place update through a view corrupts a tensor autograd saved for backward, which surfaces later as a version error.", "Two names for one memory are updated in a different order on different threads, and results race."],
    v: "Write through a view and confirm the base and sibling views reflect the change. Modify a view of a tensor saved for backward and confirm the framework either raises a version error or regenerates the history correctly.",
    dep: ["storage","shape-and-strides","version-counters"]
  },
  "storage": {
    s: "The reference-counted block of raw memory behind one or more tensors, with a size, a device, and the allocator that owns it.",
    d: "Storage is a pointer, byte size, device and a deleter, with reference counts shared by tensors that view it. When the last reference drops, the deleter returns the block to its allocator. Resizing is allowed for some uses. Storage may wrap memory the framework does not own, such as a mapped file, an array from the host language or a buffer from another library, with a custom deleter. Sharing storage across processes through shared memory allows zero-copy passing of tensors to data workers. The difficulty is lifetime across streams and processes: a block must not be reused while any asynchronous use remains.",
    k: ["Reference-counted shared storage vs unique ownership: shared counting makes views safe and cheap, but frees are nondeterministic relative to the host language's collector.", "Wrapping external memory zero-copy vs copying it in: zero-copy is fast and saves memory, but the framework must trust the other owner not to free or move it."],
    p: ["Storage handed between processes through shared memory is freed by the sender while the receiver still maps it.", "A block freed on the host side is reused while a kernel on another stream is still reading it."],
    v: "Create a tensor from external memory without copying, mutate it through the framework, and confirm the external buffer changes. Drop all references and confirm the deleter runs exactly once and the block returns to the allocator.",
    dep: ["caching-allocator","host-and-pinned-memory","device-abstraction"]
  },
  "version-counters": {
    s: "A counter on each storage that increments on every in-place modification, letting autograd detect that a tensor saved for backward was changed before its use.",
    d: "Each tensor shares a version number with all of its views. Every in-place operation increments it. When autograd saves a tensor for backward, it also records the version at that time. At backward, a mismatch means the tensor was overwritten after being saved, and the engine raises an error instead of computing a wrong gradient. The counter is shared across views so a change through one is detected through all. Operations under no-grad that modify data still bump the version. The difficulty is false alarms and misses: legitimate rewrites trip it, and paths that bypass the counter, such as direct writes to raw storage, are invisible.",
    k: ["Error on mismatch vs recompute or copy automatically: erroring is safe and explicit, but forces users to restructure code; automatic copying would hide memory cost and surprising behavior.", "Counter per storage vs per tensor: per storage catches mutation through any view, but makes unrelated views of one buffer interfere."],
    p: ["Writing to raw storage or through an external pointer bypasses the counter, and autograd computes a wrong gradient without error.", "An in-place operation on an activation in a normal layer triggers a version error that points at the backward call instead of the modification."],
    v: "Save a tensor for backward, modify it in place through a view, and confirm backward raises a version error that names the operator. Repeat without the modification and confirm gradients are correct.",
    dep: ["storage","views-and-aliasing"]
  },
  "host-and-pinned-memory": {
    s: "Host memory used for staging data, including page-locked regions that the device can read by direct memory access, which are required for asynchronous transfers.",
    d: "Ordinary host memory is pageable, so the driver must first copy it into a temporary page-locked buffer before transferring, which blocks the host. Page-locked memory is mapped for direct access by the device and allows copies to run asynchronously and overlap with compute. It is a limited resource, since the operating system cannot swap it, and allocating it is slow, so the framework keeps a caching pool. Host tensors for data loading, checkpoint staging and offload use this pool. The difficulty is that too much pinned memory harms the whole machine, and a pinned buffer must not be reused until its copy completes.",
    k: ["Cached pool of pinned buffers vs allocate per transfer: a pool avoids slow allocation, but holds scarce memory indefinitely.", "Pinned staging vs pageable direct copy: pinned allows overlap and higher bandwidth, but costs a copy into the buffer and system memory pressure."],
    p: ["A pinned buffer is recycled for the next batch while the previous asynchronous copy is still in flight, so the device receives mixed data.", "Pinning most of host memory causes the operating system to kill processes or thrash."],
    v: "Time a host-to-device copy from pageable and from pinned memory and confirm the pinned copy is faster and overlaps with a running kernel in the profile. Check that the pool's size stays bounded under a long run.",
    dep: ["storage","streams","events-and-sync"]
  },
  "caching-allocator": {
    s: "A memory allocator that keeps freed accelerator blocks for reuse instead of returning them to the driver, because driver allocation is slow and synchronizing.",
    d: "Requests are rounded to size classes and served from a pool of cached blocks; large segments obtained from the driver are split to satisfy small requests and merged when neighbors are free. Pools are kept per stream, because a block freed on one stream may still be in use by pending work on another. When a block is used on a second stream, it is recorded, and only reused after events show that the use finished. On failure to find a block, the allocator frees cached blocks and retries before raising out-of-memory. The difficulty is fragmentation, cross-stream safety, and the gap between reserved and allocated memory.",
    k: ["Caching blocks vs returning memory to the driver at once: caching makes allocation nearly free and avoids synchronizing, but reserved memory looks used to other processes and tools.", "Per-stream pools vs one shared pool: per-stream pools are safe without extra synchronization, but can strand free blocks in a stream that no longer needs them."],
    p: ["Memory freed on one stream and reused on another before pending kernels finish gives corrupted data, with no error.", "Several processes sharing a device each cache memory, and one hits out-of-memory while others hold large idle caches."],
    v: "Allocate and free tensors in a loop and confirm the driver is called only on growth, with reserved memory stable afterward. Use a tensor on a second stream, free it, and confirm the block is not reused until that stream's work completes.",
    dep: ["storage","streams","events-and-sync","allocator-fragmentation","device-abstraction"]
  },
  "allocator-fragmentation": {
    s: "The loss of usable memory when free space is split into pieces too small for new requests, so allocation can fail even when total free memory is sufficient.",
    d: "Tensors of varying sizes and lifetimes leave holes between live blocks. A request larger than any hole needs a new segment from the driver, or fails. Dynamic shapes, variable sequence lengths and interleaved short-lived and long-lived tensors worsen it. Mitigations include size-class rounding, splitting policies that avoid tiny remainders, expandable segments that grow in place by remapping virtual memory, allocating long-lived tensors from a separate pool, and static planning in compilers. Snapshots show the layout. The difficulty is that fragmentation depends on allocation order, which changes with shapes and with code changes anywhere in the program.",
    k: ["Expandable virtual-memory segments vs fixed-size segments: expandable segments reduce fragmentation by growing in place, but rely on driver features and add mapping cost.", "Aggressive block splitting vs limited splitting: aggressive splitting reuses space well but creates many tiny remainders; limited splitting wastes some space and keeps blocks mergeable."],
    p: ["A job trains for hours and then fails with out-of-memory while the snapshot shows gigabytes free in unusable fragments.", "Variable sequence lengths request a new size every step, and the pool grows without bound."],
    v: "Run a workload with varying tensor sizes for many steps and track the ratio of allocated to reserved memory and the largest free block. Confirm the settings chosen keep reserved memory stable and no out-of-memory error occurs.",
    dep: ["caching-allocator","memory-snapshots","dynamic-shapes"]
  },
  "device-abstraction": {
    s: "The interface that represents a compute device by type and index, and gives uniform operations for allocating memory, copying, synchronizing and querying properties across CPUs and accelerators.",
    d: "A device is a type and ordinal. Each device type provides a guard that sets the current device in a scope, a runtime interface for memory allocation, copies and streams, and capability queries such as memory size and supported dtypes. Tensors record their device, and operators require inputs on the same one. Copies between devices go through the best available path, direct or via host. A per-thread current device selects defaults. The difficulty is hiding differences, such as memory models and stream semantics, without losing performance, and ensuring code does not assume one device type.",
    k: ["A common device interface vs per-backend code paths in the framework: a common interface lets new hardware plug in, but forces lowest-common-denominator semantics for rare features.", "Implicit current device vs explicit device arguments everywhere: implicit is convenient, but makes bugs where an operation lands on the wrong device."],
    p: ["A tensor created without a device argument lands on the current device, which differs in a worker thread, and operations fail or silently use the wrong device.", "Code that hardcodes one device type breaks when the framework runs on another."],
    v: "Run the same test suite against each supported device type through the interface, confirming identical results within tolerance, and verify that cross-device operations raise a clear error and explicit copies succeed.",
    dep: ["streams","hardware-backend-interface","device-topology"]
  },
  "streams": {
    s: "Ordered queues of device work: operations on one stream run in order, while different streams may run concurrently, which enables overlap of compute and copies.",
    d: "Each device has a default stream and can create more. Kernels, copies and collectives are enqueued on a stream and return immediately; the host runs ahead. Within a stream, order is guaranteed; across streams, only events impose order. The framework uses separate streams for compute, host-device copies, communication and sometimes backward. Memory use across streams requires the allocator to know which streams touched a block. Each thread has a current stream. The difficulty is correctness: missing synchronization between streams leads to races that show up as rare wrong results, and too many streams hurts scheduling.",
    k: ["Multiple streams for overlap vs a single stream for simplicity: multiple streams hide copy and communication time, but make races possible and require careful event use.", "Stream per thread vs shared stream: per-thread removes contention for launching, but changes ordering guarantees across threads."],
    p: ["A tensor produced on one stream is consumed on another without waiting, and the consumer sometimes reads incomplete data.", "Using the default stream's implicit synchronization by accident serializes work that was meant to overlap."],
    v: "Enqueue a long kernel on one stream and a copy on another with no dependency and confirm they overlap in the profile. Add an event dependency and confirm the second starts only after the first ends.",
    dep: ["device-abstraction","events-and-sync","async-launch-queue"]
  },
  "events-and-sync": {
    s: "Markers recorded in a stream that other streams or the host can wait on, giving fine-grained ordering without stopping the whole device.",
    d: "An event is recorded at a point in a stream and completes when all prior work in that stream finishes. Another stream can wait on the event before running later work, which creates a dependency across streams without blocking the host. The host can query or block on an event, or synchronize a whole stream or device, which stalls it until the device catches up. Events time kernels, mark checkpoints for memory reuse by the allocator, and sequence communication against compute. The difficulty is overuse of host-blocking synchronization, which removes the host's lead over the device, and forgotten dependencies, which cause races.",
    k: ["Event waits between streams vs host-side synchronization: event waits keep the host free to enqueue ahead, while host synchronization is simple but stalls launching.", "Many fine-grained events vs few coarse ones: fine-grained events maximize overlap, but add overhead and complexity."],
    p: ["A hidden synchronization, such as reading a tensor value on the host each step, cuts the host's lead and leaves the device idle between steps.", "Waiting on an event that was never recorded passes immediately, so the dependency is silently absent."],
    v: "Record an event after a long kernel, wait on it from a second stream, and confirm the second stream's work starts after the first ends in the profile. Count host synchronizations per step and confirm there are none outside logging steps.",
    dep: ["streams","device-abstraction"]
  },
  "async-launch-queue": {
    s: "The mechanism by which the host enqueues kernel launches and returns immediately, running ahead of the device until a queue limit or a synchronization point.",
    d: "Each launch writes a command into a device queue and returns in microseconds, long before the kernel runs. The host can therefore prepare many steps' worth of work while the device executes, as long as it never needs a result. The queue has a finite depth, so a host that gets too far ahead blocks. Errors in a kernel are reported later, at a subsequent call or synchronization, which makes stack traces misleading. A mode that synchronizes after every launch trades speed for exact error locations. The difficulty is host-bound workloads, where launches take longer than kernels, so the device starves.",
    k: ["Asynchronous launches vs synchronous execution: asynchronous keeps the device busy and overlaps host work, but delays error reports away from their source.", "Deep queue vs shallow queue: a deep queue hides host jitter, but increases memory held by in-flight work and delays error discovery."],
    p: ["A device-side error is reported at an unrelated later operation, and engineers debug the wrong line.", "Printing a tensor inside the training loop forces a synchronization every step and removes the speedup from asynchronous launches."],
    v: "Time a loop of launches with and without synchronization after each. Confirm the asynchronous loop returns control long before the device finishes, and that blocking mode reports an injected kernel error at the exact call.",
    dep: ["streams","device-abstraction","eager-execution"]
  },
  "hardware-backend-interface": {
    s: "The extension point through which a new accelerator plugs in: it supplies a device type, memory and stream runtime, kernels, and optionally a compiler backend, without modifying the framework core.",
    d: "A backend registers a device type and implements the device interface: allocation, copies, streams, events, and synchronization, then registers kernels for the dispatch key of its device, starting with a minimal set and relying on fallbacks and composites for the rest. A compiler backend can additionally accept captured graphs and lower them directly to the hardware's own program format. Collective communication and profiler hooks are optional extensions. The backend is built as a separate library loaded at runtime. The difficulty is the operator surface, since thousands of operators need kernels or acceptable fallbacks, and the interface changes between framework releases.",
    k: ["Out-of-tree backend vs in-tree support: out-of-tree evolves on the vendor's schedule without core review, but breaks when the framework's internal interfaces change.", "Per-operator kernels vs whole-graph compiler backend: per-operator is incremental and debuggable, while a graph backend can optimize holistically but must cover capture limitations."],
    p: ["A backend implements the common operators but not the long tail, and models that use one rare operator fail or silently fall back to the host.", "An internal interface change in a framework release breaks the backend's build, delaying hardware support for months."],
    v: "Run the framework's operator conformance suite against the backend and report coverage and pass rate, then train a reference model for a fixed number of steps and compare its loss curve with the same model on a reference device.",
    dep: ["device-abstraction","operator-registry","dispatch-keys","fallbacks-and-composites","streams"]
  },
  "random-number-generators": {
    s: "Per-device pseudo-random generators with explicit state, producing the random values for initialization, dropout and sampling, with seeding and state capture for reproducibility.",
    d: "Each device has a generator holding a seed and an offset. Host generators use sequential algorithms; accelerator generators use counter-based ones, where each thread computes its values from the seed, offset and its own index, so many threads draw independent values without sharing state. Each random kernel advances the offset by the number of values it consumed, which is recorded so that a replay, such as activation recomputation, can restore the same stream. Generator state is saved in checkpoints. The difficulty is that identical results require identical call order and kernel launch shapes, and distributed training needs both shared and per-rank streams.",
    k: ["Counter-based generators vs sequential generators on devices: counter-based allow parallel independent draws and easy replay, but results depend on the launch configuration.", "Same seed on all ranks vs different seed per rank: the same seed keeps replicated operations such as dropout on replicated activations consistent, while different seeds give independent data augmentation."],
    p: ["Recomputed activations draw different random numbers because the generator's offset was not restored, so gradients do not match the forward.", "Every data worker starts with the same seed and yields identical augmentation."],
    v: "Seed, draw values, save the generator state, draw more, restore the state and draw again, and confirm the two later draws are identical. Check that different ranks produce different streams where intended and identical ones where required.",
    dep: ["device-abstraction","accelerator-kernels"]
  },
  "device-topology": {
    s: "The framework's model of how devices relate: which share a host, which have direct links, memory affinity to host processors, and the bandwidth between each pair.",
    d: "At startup the runtime queries the driver for links between devices, the host processors and memory nearest to each device, and the network adapters attached to each. This gives a graph with bandwidths. The framework uses it to enable direct peer access, to pin each rank's data loader to nearby processors and memory, to build communication rings and trees and to place parallel groups. Wrong placement of a process relative to its device can halve host-to-device bandwidth. The difficulty is that topology is queried from drivers that are inexact, differs across machine models, and is easy to ignore until a job is slow.",
    k: ["Query topology at run time vs rely on scheduler configuration: querying adapts to any machine, but the data is only as good as the driver; configuration is explicit but drifts from reality.", "Bind each rank to nearby host processors vs let the operating system schedule: binding gives stable bandwidth and latency, but is wrong if the topology report is wrong."],
    p: ["Ranks are bound to processors far from their device, and host-to-device copies run at half speed without any error.", "Peer access is enabled between devices with no direct link, and traffic silently takes a slow path."],
    v: "Print the discovered topology for a node and compare it with the vendor's documented layout, then measure host-to-device and device-to-device bandwidth for each pair, confirming they match the reported tiers.",
    dep: ["device-abstraction","host-thread-pools"]
  },
  "host-thread-pools": {
    s: "The pools of host threads that run intra-operator parallel loops, inter-operator tasks and backward work, whose sizes must be set to avoid oversubscribing cores.",
    d: "One pool parallelizes the inside of a large host operator, splitting a loop across cores. Another runs independent operators concurrently where the program allows. The backward engine adds a thread per device. Data loader workers and the host language's own threads add more. The sizes default to the number of cores, which oversubscribes the machine when combined, and math libraries bring their own pools. Thread affinity and fork behavior matter, since worker processes created by forking inherit a pool's state without its threads. The difficulty is coordinating several independent pools and avoiding deadlock after fork.",
    k: ["Shared pool across libraries vs separate pools per library: shared avoids oversubscription, but couples libraries to a common runtime; separate pools are independent but multiply threads.", "Fewer threads per operator vs more: fewer leaves cores for data loading and other processes, more speeds up large host operations."],
    p: ["Several libraries each start a thread per core and the machine is oversubscribed, so every step slows and timings become noisy.", "A pool is used in a forked worker process and deadlocks because its threads did not survive the fork."],
    v: "Count threads in a running training process and confirm the total matches the intended budget. Sweep intra-operator threads on a host-bound workload and confirm that throughput peaks near the core count available to the process.",
    dep: ["device-topology"]
  },
  "numerical-policy": {
    s: "The decisions about which precision each operation uses, how accumulation is done, which fast approximations are allowed and what error is acceptable, which every layer inherits.",
    d: "Policy sets storage dtypes, accumulation dtypes inside reductions and matrix multiplies, whether fast lower-precision matrix modes are enabled for nominally full-precision work, which operators stay in high precision, and tolerance levels for tests. It touches kernels, autocast, the optimizer, communication reductions and quantization. Floating-point addition is not associative, so any change of parallelization, fusion or algorithm shifts low bits. The hard part is that errors compound over millions of steps, and what looks like a harmless local approximation can show up only as slow divergence or a worse final metric.",
    k: ["Fast approximate modes by default vs exact by default: fast modes give large speedups for little visible error, but silently differ from the reference; exact modes are slower but easier to validate.", "Tight test tolerances vs loose: tight catches regressions early but fails on legitimate platform differences; loose passes everywhere but admits real bugs."],
    p: ["A fast matrix mode is enabled globally and a model with sensitive layers degrades only after days of training.", "Reductions in communication done in low precision lose small gradient contributions at large rank counts."],
    v: "Run a reference model under each policy setting and compare final metrics and loss curves against a full-precision run over the whole training schedule, not a handful of steps, with a stated tolerance.",
    dep: []
  },
  "determinism": {
    s: "Whether two runs with the same inputs and seed give the same results, bit for bit, which constrains kernels, reductions, scheduling, communication and data order across the whole stack.",
    d: "Sources of nondeterminism include atomic accumulation order, autotuned algorithm choice, parallel reduction trees whose shape depends on thread timing, collective algorithms that vary, data worker timing, and different device counts. Making a run deterministic means choosing fixed-order kernels, disabling timing-based selection, fixing data order and seeding all generators, which usually costs speed. Different hardware or world size cannot be bitwise identical. Most teams aim for determinism on a fixed configuration to bisect bugs, and statistical equivalence elsewhere. The difficulty is that determinism must be designed into every operator and tested continuously, since one new nondeterministic kernel breaks it.",
    k: ["Strict bitwise determinism vs statistical reproducibility: strict allows exact bisection of regressions at a speed cost; statistical allows faster kernels but cannot prove two changes equivalent.", "Determinism as a mode vs always on: a mode lets production run fast, but the mode needs its own testing to remain valid."],
    p: ["A new kernel using atomics is added and silently breaks determinism, which is noticed weeks later when a bisect fails.", "Resuming on a different device count changes reduction order, so the resumed loss does not match the original."],
    v: "Run an identical job twice with deterministic settings and compare loss every step and final weights bitwise. Add the test to continuous integration so a nondeterministic kernel fails the build.",
    dep: []
  },
  "asynchrony": {
    s: "How much work runs ahead of, or alongside, other work: host ahead of device, communication overlapping compute, data loading overlapping steps, which shapes performance and makes errors and timings appear late.",
    d: "Nearly every layer is asynchronous: kernel launches return before they run, copies and collectives run on their own streams, data loaders prefetch, and checkpoints write in the background. This hides latency and is the main source of speed. It also moves failures away from their cause, since an error appears at a later call, makes wall-clock timing meaningless without synchronization, and creates races when a dependency is missing. Every stage needs explicit completion tracking through events. The hard part is that correctness depends on ordering that is invisible in the program text.",
    k: ["Maximum asynchrony vs frequent synchronization: maximum asynchrony hides latency and keeps the device busy, but makes debugging and timing hard; frequent synchronization is easy to reason about but slow.", "Debug mode with synchronous launches vs production configuration: a synchronous mode finds errors precisely, but may hide races that only occur at full speed."],
    p: ["Benchmarks time only the enqueue and report impossible speeds because they never waited for the device to finish.", "A missing dependency between streams works for months until a change in timing exposes the race."],
    v: "Time a step with explicit device synchronization before and after, and compare with the profile's device busy time. Run with forced synchronous launches and confirm results are identical to the asynchronous run.",
    dep: []
  },
  "memory-budget": {
    s: "How accelerator memory is divided among parameters, gradients, optimizer state, activations, communication buffers and caches, a constraint that drives the choice of parallelism and recomputation.",
    d: "Memory use per device is roughly the sum of weights, gradients, optimizer state, which for adaptive methods exceeds the weights several times, saved activations, which scale with batch and sequence length, temporary workspaces, communicator buffers and allocator overhead. For inference, weights and the key-value cache dominate. Each technique trades another resource for memory: sharding for communication, recomputation for compute, offload for transfer time, quantization for accuracy. The hard part is that memory is a hard wall, unlike speed, so a job that fits at one batch size fails with out-of-memory at the next.",
    k: ["Plan memory analytically before running vs find limits by trial: analysis predicts feasibility and guides parallelism, but ignores allocator overhead; trial is accurate but wastes cluster time on failed launches.", "Headroom reserved for fragmentation vs using nearly all memory: headroom avoids late failures, but leaves capacity unused."],
    p: ["A model fits on the first step and runs out of memory on a later step when a longer sequence arrives.", "Activations are counted but communication buffers and workspaces are not, and the estimate is off by many gigabytes."],
    v: "Estimate memory by component for a configuration, run it, and compare the estimate with measured peak allocated and reserved memory per device, confirming agreement within a few percent for each component.",
    dep: []
  },
  "dynamic-shapes": {
    s: "The degree to which tensor sizes vary between calls, such as batch, sequence length or data-dependent counts, which affects capture, compilation, allocation, graph replay and batching.",
    d: "Static shapes allow whole-graph compilation, static memory plans, launch graph replay and tuned kernels with known tile sizes. Real workloads vary: sequence lengths differ per request, batches shrink at the end of an epoch, and some operators produce data-dependent sizes. Frameworks respond with padding to buckets, symbolic dimensions, recompilation per distinct shape and fallbacks to eager execution. Each choice shifts cost among compile time, run time and memory. The hard part is that shape variation interacts with every layer: guards, cached kernels, fragmentation and communication sizes.",
    k: ["Pad to a few fixed sizes vs fully dynamic: padding keeps compiled artifacts and graph replay valid with wasted compute; dynamic avoids waste but loses specialization.", "Recompile on new shapes vs symbolic compile: recompiling gives the fastest code per shape, while symbolic compiles once with slightly slower code and more complex guards."],
    p: ["A new sequence length arrives and triggers a multi-minute recompile in the middle of serving traffic.", "A last partial batch has a new shape and triggers recompilation or an allocator growth only at the end of each epoch."],
    v: "Feed a recorded mix of input shapes through the system and record compile counts, cache sizes, padding waste and memory growth. Confirm all stay within the stated bounds across the whole run.",
    dep: []
  },
  "compatibility-versioning": {
    s: "How long saved checkpoints, exported graphs, compiled caches and extension binaries remain loadable and give the same results across framework, compiler, driver and hardware versions.",
    d: "Artifacts embed assumptions: operator versions in exported graphs, tensor names in checkpoints, kernel binaries compiled for a device generation, and extension code built against internal interfaces. The framework versions operator schemas, keeps behavior for deprecated versions, and adds upgraders that rewrite old graphs. Cache keys include library versions. Backward compatibility prevents breakage but accumulates code and constrains improvements. Semantics changes, such as a corrected numerical default, are the hardest, since old models then compute differently with no error. The hard part is testing against years of old artifacts.",
    k: ["Strict long-term compatibility vs freedom to change behavior: compatibility protects deployed models and users, but keeps old mistakes alive and slows cleanup.", "Version each operator vs version the whole framework: per-operator versions allow precise upgrades, but need upgrade logic for every change."],
    p: ["A change to a default numerical behavior makes an old exported model produce slightly different answers with no warning.", "A compiled cache from before a driver update is reused and crashes in a kernel built for the old version."],
    v: "Keep a corpus of checkpoints and exported graphs from past releases, load each in the current release in continuous integration, and compare outputs with recorded reference values, failing the build on any difference beyond tolerance.",
    dep: []
  },
});
